**Java Implementation**
```java
public class HtmlElement {
    private String tagName;
    private String content;

    public HtmlElement(String tagName, String content) {
        this.tagName = tagName;
        this.content = content;
    }

    public String getTagName() {
        return tagName;
    }

    public void setTagName(String tagName) {
        this.tagName = tagName;
    }

    public String getContent() {
        return content;
    }

    public void setContent(String content) {
        this.content = content;
    }

    public String escapeHtml() {
        return content.replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;");
    }
}

public class HtmlDocument {
    private List<HtmlElement> elements;

    public HtmlDocument() {
        elements = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void addElement(HtmlElement element) {
        elements.add(element);
    }

    public String generateHtml() {
        StringBuilder html = new StringBuilder();
        for (HtmlElement element : elements) {
            html.append("<").append(element.getTagName()).append(">").append(element.getContent().replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;")).append("</").append(element.getTagName()).append(">");
        }
        return html.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HtmlDocument document = new HtmlDocument();
        HtmlElement element1 = new HtmlElement("div", "<script>alert('XSS')</script>");
        HtmlElement element2 = new HtmlElement("p", "Hello World");
        document.addElement(element1);
        document.addElement(element2);

        System.out.println(document.generateHtml());
    }
}
```

**Explanation**

This Java implementation defines two classes: `HtmlElement` and `HtmlDocument`. The `HtmlElement` class represents an HTML element with a tag name and content. It provides methods to get the tag name, set the tag name, get the content, and set the content. The `escapeHtml()` method escapes special characters in the content.

The `HtmlDocument` class represents an HTML document and contains a list of elements. It provides a method to add elements to the document and generate the HTML content by iterating through the elements and escaping special characters.

**Example Output**

When you run the `main()` method, it creates a new `HtmlDocument`, adds two `HtmlElement`s (a `div` element with a script tag and a `p` element with text), and prints the generated HTML content.

The output will be:
```
<div><script>alert(&#x27;XSS&#x27;)</script></div><p>Hello World</p>
```

**Generating Outer HTML**

To generate outer HTML, you can modify the `generateHtml()` method to include the opening and closing tags for each element. Here's an updated implementation:
